        <!-- INTRODUCTION -->

        <section class="opening-section">

          <p>
            Many years ago, when I was first seriously learning about
            homeopathy, I came across an article by the late homeopathy
            historian Julian Winston about the history of homeopathy during
            epidemics.
          </p>

          <p>
            I was fascinated.
          </p>

          <p>
            I had already experienced homeopathy personally, but reading about
            its history opened another door for me. Here was a system of
            medicine founded upon a principle—the Law of Similars—that claimed
            to work with the body's own response to illness rather than simply
            suppressing symptoms. It was gentle enough that avoiding
            unnecessary harm was built into its philosophy, yet its history
            contained reports involving hospitals, epidemics, thousands of
            patients, government records, military units, physicians and
            entire communities.
          </p>

          <p>
            That mattered to me.
          </p>

          <p>
            The more I learned, the more convinced I became that homeopathy
            was not simply a collection of “natural remedies.” It was a
            complete system of medicine, founded upon a natural law that
            Samuel Hahnemann believed could be observed repeatedly:
            <em> similia similibus curentur</em>—let likes be cured by likes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Years later, as a student of classical homeopathy, I returned to
            those epidemic stories with different questions.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where did these numbers actually come from? What did the original
            physicians report? Were the statistics preserved accurately? Were
            there government records, hospital returns, medical journals or
            contemporary investigations behind the stories?
          </p>

          <p>
            I began following the references backward.
          </p>

          <p>
            What I found was more complicated than a collection of remarkable
            statistics—but also far more interesting.
          </p>

        </section>


        <!-- HAHNEMANN -->

        <section>
          <p class="section-date">1799–1831</p>

          <h2>Hahnemann and Epidemic Disease</h2>

          <p>
            Epidemic disease occupied Dr. Samuel Hahnemann's attention from very
            early in the development of homeopathy.
          </p>

          <p>
            His approach was not simply to attach a particular medicine to the
            name of a disease. Instead, he taught that an epidemic should be
            studied through the symptoms appearing across multiple affected
            individuals.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the <em> Organon of the Medical Art</em>, particularly §100–102,
            Hahnemann explained that the complete character of an epidemic
            disease often becomes apparent only after observing several cases.
            Each patient contributes part of the picture.
          </p>

          <p>
            Taken together, those cases reveal the characteristic symptom
            pattern of the epidemic.
          </p>

          <p>
            What later homeopaths came to call the
            <em> genus epidemicus</em> is the medicine whose known symptom
            picture most closely corresponds to that collective picture.
          </p>

          <div class="principle-box">
            <p class="principle-label">The Epidemic Picture</p>

            <p>
              The individual cases are not discarded. They are studied
              together. Symptoms shared across the epidemic, along with
              unusual and characteristic features, gradually reveal the
              collective disease picture.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            This distinction remains important. Hahnemann's method of epidemic
            prescribing should not be confused with assigning a predetermined
            medicine or nosode to a disease simply because that disease has
            been named.
          </p>

        </section>


        <!-- SCARLET FEVER -->

<section>
  <p class="section-date">1799–1801</p>

  <h2>Scarlet Fever and Belladonna</h2>

  <div class="document-layout figure-left">

    <figure class="historical-document">
      <img
        src="/images/history/hahnemann-scarlet-fever-870-871.jpg"
        alt="Pages 870 and 871 of Samuel Hahnemann's Lesser Writings showing his account of the scarlet fever epidemic"
        loading="lazy"
      />

      <figcaption>
        <strong>
          Dr. Samuel Hahnemann's account of the scarlet-fever epidemic of 1799.
        </strong>
        <em> Cure and Prevention of Scarlet Fever</em>, originally published
        in 1801 and later translated into English in R. E. Dudgeon's
        <em> The Lesser Writings of Samuel Hahnemann</em>, <br>pp. 870–871.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="document-text">

      <p>
        One of the earliest and most famous examples arose during an
        epidemic of scarlet fever.
      </p>

      <p>
        Dr. Hahnemann's account appeared in
        <em> Cure and Prevention of Scarlet Fever</em>, published as a
        pamphlet at Gotha in 1801 and later included in R. E. Dudgeon's
        English translation of Hahnemann's
        <em> Lesser Writings</em>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hahnemann described an epidemic beginning in 1799 and the
        observations that led him to investigate Belladonna in relation
        to the prevailing illness.
      </p>

      <p>
        His reasoning was explicitly grounded in similarity: Belladonna
        was capable of producing symptoms resembling those he was
        observing in the epidemic.
      </p>

      <p>
        The context matters. Hahnemann was not proposing Belladonna as a
        routine remedy to be taken indefinitely because scarlet fever
        existed somewhere in the world. His observations and preventive
        use occurred in the presence of an actual, prevailing epidemic and
        direct exposure to the disease.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hahnemann's later writings also demonstrate why the disease name
        alone was insufficient. He distinguished the scarlet fever for
        which Belladonna had corresponded from another eruptive epidemic
        requiring a different remedy.
      </p>

      <p>
        The epidemic had to be observed.
      </p>

      <p>
        The remedy had to correspond to the disease actually appearing
        in that time and place.
      </p>

    </div>

  </div>
</section>


        <!-- CHOLERA / INVISIBLE LIVING CREATURES -->

<section>
  <p class="section-date">1831</p>

  <h2>Cholera and “Invisible, Living Creatures”</h2>

  <div class="document-layout figure-right">

    <div class="document-text">

      <p>
        When Asiatic cholera moved across Europe in 1831, Hahnemann wrote
        extensively about its treatment and propagation.
      </p>

      <p>
        One passage is particularly striking.
      </p>

      <p>
        At a time when many believed that cholera was from poisonous vapors in the air, 
        Hahnemann argued that its rapid spread was connected with:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        “an enormously increased brood of those excessively minute,
        invisible, living creatures”
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        He went on to describe the contagious matter surrounding the sick
        as containing what he believed were probably millions of these
        living agents.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hahnemann did not discover{" "}
        <em>Vibrio cholerae</em>, and his explanation contained other ideas that
        would not survive later scientific investigation.
      </p>

      <p>
        Nevertheless, in 1831—decades before bacteriology established the
        organism responsible for cholera—he rejected a purely atmospheric
        explanation and proposed that cholera was spread by extraordinarily
        small, invisible living agents.
      </p>

      <p>
        The underlying infectious-agent concept was remarkably
        forward-looking.
      </p>

    </div>

    <figure class="historical-document hahnemann-document">
      <img
        src="/images/history/hahnemann-cholera-758-759.jpg"
        alt="Pages 758 and 759 of Samuel Hahnemann's Lesser Writings discussing invisible living agents in the propagation of cholera"
        loading="lazy"
      />

      <figcaption>
        <strong>
          Hahnemann on the mode of propagation of Asiatic cholera, 1831.
        </strong>{" "}
        From{" "}
        <em>The Lesser Writings of Samuel Hahnemann</em>, translated by
        R. E. Dudgeon, pp. 758–759.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

  </div>
</section>


        <!-- CINCINNATI -->

<section>
  <p class="section-date">1849</p>

  <h2>The Cincinnati Cholera Epidemic</h2>

  <div class="document-layout figure-right">

    <div class="document-text">

      <p>
        Cholera returned repeatedly during the nineteenth century, leaving
        behind some of the most frequently cited statistics in the history
        of homeopathy.
      </p>

      <p>
        Cincinnati's 1849 epidemic is one of the better documented
        examples.
      </p>

      <p>
        Benjamin F. Joslin's 1854
        <em> The Homoeopathic Treatment of Epidemic Cholera</em> records the
        work of Drs. Pulte and Ehrmann in Cincinnati.
      </p>

      <p>
        Joslin reported:
      </p>

      <div class="stat-card">
        <p class="stat-number">1,116</p>
        <p class="stat-label">developed cholera cases</p>

        <div class="stat-divider"></div>

        <p class="stat-number secondary">35 deaths</p>
        <p class="stat-label">approximately 3.1%</p>
      </div>

      <p>
        Joslin separately reported 1,350 cases of cholerine or diarrhea,
        all of whom were said to have recovered.
      </p>

      <p>
        That distinction is important because later retellings sometimes
        combined the two categories, which produced a misleading total number of cases
        and an even lower mortality percentage.
      </p>

      <p>
        Joslin himself explicitly rejected that approach.
      </p>

      <p>
        Immediately before presenting the Cincinnati statistics, he wrote
        that combining diarrhea and choleroid (less severe illnesses or early stage cholera) cases with developed cholera
        would reduce the percentage of deaths, but:
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        “It would not however be fair to take advantage of such a
        combination, in estimating the relative success of the two
        systems.”
      </blockquote>

    </div>

    <figure class="historical-document joslin-document">
      <img
        src="/images/history/joslin-cincinnati-1849.jpg"
        alt="Benjamin F. Joslin's 1854 account showing 1,116 cholera cases and 35 deaths in Cincinnati"
        loading="lazy"
      />

      <figcaption>
          Benjamin F. Joslin's 1854 account of the Cincinnati cholera epidemic.
        
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

  </div>
</section>


        <!-- ROUTH -->

<section>
  <p class="section-date">1852</p>

  <h2>Frederick Routh and a Contested Comparison</h2>

  <div class="routh-layout">

    <div class="routh-copy">
      <p>
        Not all nineteenth-century comparisons were accepted without
        dispute.
      </p>

      <p>
        In 1852, physician Frederick Routh published
        <em> Fallacies of Homoeopathy</em>, a work expressly critical of
        homeopathy. In examining cholera statistics, Routh reproduced a
        Bavarian comparison in which conventional treatment recorded 2,299
        cases and 1,134 deaths—a mortality of approximately 49.3%—while
        homeopathic treatment recorded 558 cases and 25 deaths, or approximately
        4.4%.
      </p>

      <div class="routh-stat-card">
        <div class="routh-stat">
          <span class="routh-stat-label">Conventional treatment</span>
          <strong>49.3%</strong>
          <span>1,134 deaths / 2,299 cases</span>
        </div>

        <div class="routh-stat-divider"></div>

        <div class="routh-stat">
          <span class="routh-stat-label">Homeopathic treatment</span>
          <strong>4.4%</strong>
          <span>25 deaths / 558 cases</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        The comparison is particularly noteworthy because Dr. Routh himself
        identified the <strong> Bavarian reports as belonging to the same epidemic</strong>.
        The figures therefore did not represent unrelated cholera outbreaks
        separated by different places or historical periods.
      </p>

      <p>
        Routh nevertheless disputed the conclusion that treatment necessarily
        explained the extraordinary difference. He argued that the reports
        could represent different periods within the same epidemic and, questioned whether less severe
        cases may have been included among the homeopathic returns.
      </p>

      <p>
        This makes Dr. Routh's account especially valuable historically. These
        striking statistics were not confined to homeopathic literature: they
        were reproduced by a contemporary physician writing expressly against
        homeopathy, demonstrating the scrutiny homeopathy has endured.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="routh-source">
      <img
        src="/images/history/routh-74-75.jpg"
        alt="Pages from Frederick Routh's 1852 critique of homeopathy showing cholera mortality comparisons and his discussion of the Bavarian statistics"
        loading="lazy"
      />

      <figcaption>
        Dr. Frederick Routh, 1852 <br><em>Fallacies of Homoeopathy</em>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

  </div>
</section>


        <!-- LONDON -->

        <section>
          <p class="section-date">1854</p>

          <h2>London: A Government Investigation</h2>

          <p>
            The London cholera epidemic of 1854 produced one of the most
            important surviving comparisons.
          </p>

          <p>
            The British government collected returns from hospitals treating
            cholera patients. The word “returns” referred to standardized clinical records submitted to the government's Medical Council. 
            Hospitals recorded individual cholera cases on printed forms according to the stage of disease, documenting patient information, treatment and outcome. 
            The completed forms could then be compiled into statistical tables comparing the results reported by different hospitals and methods of treatment.
            Among them was the London Homoeopathic Hospital.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the more serious stages of cholera—Forms B, stages 3–5—the
            completed returns recorded:
          </p>


          <div class="historical-table-wrap">
            <p class="table-title">London Cholera Returns — 1854</p>

            <table class="historical-table">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th>Treatment setting</th>
                  <th>Cases</th>
                  <th>Deaths</th>
                  <th>Mortality</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>

              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>London Homoeopathic Hospital</td>
                  <td>87</td>
                  <td>16</td>
                  <td><strong>18.4%</strong></td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                  <td>Other reporting hospitals</td>
                  <td>3,188</td>
                  <td>1,467</td>
                  <td><strong>46.0%</strong></td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>

            <p class="table-note">
              Completed returns for serious cholera, stages 3–5. Historical
              data from the Medical Council and House of Commons returns.
            </p>
          </div>


          <p>
            The government inspector assigned to the homeopathy hospital,
            Dr. David Macloughlin, was not an advocate of homeopathy. Yet after
            examining the patients and records, he acknowledged that the cases
            being treated there were genuine cholera.
          </p>

          <p>
            The results from the London Homoeopathic Hospital were not included
            in the Medical Council's principal statistical presentation.
            Parliamentary intervention ultimately resulted in their
            preservation in a separate return.
          </p>

          <p>
            That history matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            It means the figures are not merely a later story repeated within
            homeopathy. They survive through the governmental investigation
            itself.
          </p>

        </section>


