    <div class="document-text">

      <p>
        there were 200 cases of cholera and 15 of the patients had
        reached what it described as the{" "}
        <em>ultimo grado</em>—the most severe or final stage of the
        disease. However, all 200 patients recovered.
      </p>

      <p>
        It further stated that Rubini attended the institution daily and
        was summoned at other times for serious cases.
      </p>

      <p>
        Dr. Rubini's method during this epidemic was distinctive. His treatment centered heavily upon a preparation he 
        called camphorated alcohol—a saturated solution of camphor in highly rectified alcohol—and became increasingly standardized around it. 
        Rubini described preparing it by dissolving equal weights of camphor and highly rectified alcohol to complete saturation.
      </p>

      <p>
        For that reason, I would not present Rubini’s work as a textbook example of individualized epidemic prescribing using the collective epidemic picture. It nevertheless represents an important historical episode grounded in the principles of homeopathy, especially the Law of Similars.
      </p>

      <p>
  A separate institutional report, dated November 6, documented the
  experience among women at the same institution, who were not under
  Rubini's homeopathic treatment.
</p>

<p>
  The report recorded 117 women affected by cholera. Sixty-six remained
  within the Reale Albergo, where 14 deaths were reported, while 51 were
  transferred to Santa Maria della Vita, where 15 deaths were reported.
</p>

<p>
  Altogether, the report recorded <br>29 deaths among the 117 women.
</p>


