        <!-- YELLOW FEVER -->

<section>
  <p class="section-date">1878</p>

  <h2>Yellow Fever in the American South</h2>

  <p>
    The yellow fever epidemic of 1878 devastated communities across
    the Mississippi Valley and American South.
  </p>

  <p>
    A report preserved in the
    <em> Transactions of the Homoeopathic Medical Society of the State
    of Pennsylvania</em> compiled figures from physicians practicing
    homeopathy during the epidemic.
  </p>

  <p>
    For the epidemic of 1878, the report recorded:
  </p>

  <div class="yellow-fever-layout">

    <div class="yellow-fever-copy">

      <div class="stat-card">
        <p class="stat-number">3,914</p>
        <p class="stat-label">
          reported yellow fever cases treated homeopathically
        </p>

        <div class="stat-divider"></div>

        <p class="stat-number secondary">261 deaths</p>
        <p class="stat-label">6.6% reported mortality</p>
      </div>

      <p>
        The report also placed the 1878 experience within a broader
        historical record. It included 555 cases with 33 deaths from
        the epidemic of 1853 and another 2,100 cases with 66 deaths
        from several smaller epidemics between 1853 and 1878.
      </p>

      <p>
        Combining these reports, the authors gave a grand total of
        6,569 cases treated by homeopathic physicians, with 360 deaths.
      </p>

    </div>

    <figure class="historical-document yellow-fever-source">
      <img
        src="/images/history/yellow-fever-1878.jpg"
