        <!-- INFLUENZA -->

<section>
  <p class="section-date">1918–1921</p>

  <h2>The Influenza Pandemic</h2>

  <p>
  The influenza pandemic of 1918–1919 was one of the deadliest infectious
  disease events of the modern era. It spread around the world in successive
  waves, overwhelming hospitals and communities and causing tens of millions
  of deaths worldwide. In the United States alone, hundreds of thousands died.
</p>

<p>
  Against that extraordinary background, physicians from different medical
  traditions documented their experiences treating the sick. Among the
  surviving homeopathic accounts is a remarkable collection published by
  Willis Alonzo Dewey, M.D., Professor of Materia Medica and Therapeutics and Clinical Professor of Nervous Diseases.
</p>


<p>
In 1921, W. A. Dewey, M.D., a longtime professor in the University of Michigan's Homoeopathic Medical College, 
published <em> Homeopathy in Influenza—A Chorus of Fifty in Harmony</em> in the <em> Journal of the American Institute of Homeopathy</em>.
</p>


<p>
  Dewey's article was not a clinical trial. As its title,
  <em> A Chorus of Fifty in Harmony</em>, suggests, it was a collection of
  reports from physicians describing their experiences during the pandemic.
  Their accounts came from private practices, hospitals, military settings
  and workplaces across the United States.
</p>

<p>
  Among them, Dr. Dewey reported that Dean W. A. Pearson of Philadelphia
  had collected:
</p>

  <div class="dewey-primary">

  <figure class="historical-document dewey-document-right">
    <img
      src="/images/history/dewey-influenza-1921-p1038.jpg"
      alt="Page 1038 of W. A. Dewey's 1921 article Homeopathy in Influenza—A Chorus of Fifty in Harmony"
      loading="lazy"
    />

    <figcaption>
      <strong>
        Opening page of W. A. Dewey's
        <em> Homeopathy in Influenza—A Chorus of Fifty in Harmony</em>.
      </strong>{" "}
      <em>Journal of the American Institute of Homeopathy</em>,
      May 1921, p. 1038.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="stat-card dewey-stat-card">
    <p class="stat-number">26,795</p>
    <p class="stat-label">
      influenza cases treated by physicians practicing homeopathy
    </p>

    <div class="stat-divider"></div>

    <p class="stat-number secondary">1.05%</p>
    <p class="stat-label">reported mortality</p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The figure appears on the opening page of Dewey's article alongside
    individual reports from other physicians concerning their experiences during
    the pandemic.
  </p>

  <p>
    On the following page, Dewey reproduced another collection of reports
    from Connecticut. Thirty physicians had responded to a request for data,
    reporting 6,602 cases with 55 deaths—approximately 0.83%.
  </p>

</div>

<div class="dewey-continuation">

  <figure class="historical-document dewey-document-left">
    <img
      src="/images/history/dewey-influenza-1921-p1039.jpg"
      alt="Page 1039 of W. A. Dewey's 1921 article containing reports from physicians during the influenza pandemic"
      loading="lazy"
    />

    <figcaption>
      <strong>Continuing reports collected by Dewey.</strong>{" "}
      <em>Journal of the American Institute of Homeopathy </em>,
      May 1921, p. 1039.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Other accounts described individual practices, hospitals, military
    settings and workplaces, illustrating the varied sources from which
    Dewey assembled his 1921 compilation.
  </p>

  <p>
  Among the reports collected by Dewey was the experience of a large
  industrial plant in Chicago employing approximately 8,000 people during
  the influenza epidemic. Dr. Frank Wieland reported only one death among
  the workforce and stated that Gelsemium was practically the only remedy
  used. The figure is striking, but the original wording is important:
  the 8,000 represented the total plant population, not 8,000 documented
  cases of influenza.
  </p>

  <p>
    Dewey and several physicians also expressed strong concern about
    the heavy use of aspirin and other medications during the pandemic.
    Those statements are important as contemporary medical opinion,
    but they should not be transformed into modern causal proof that
    aspirin explains differences in mortality.
  </p>

</div>

</section>


       <!-- COVID -->

<section>
  <p class="section-date">2020–2024</p>

  <h2 class="modern-data-title">
    From Historical Records to Modern Clinical Data
  </h2>

  <p>
    The methods of documentation have changed, but the effort to preserve
    clinical experience has not.
  </p>

  <p>
    Joslin, Routh, Rubini and Dewey recorded the experiences of physicians
    using the tools available in their own eras—case reports, hospital
    records, mortality statistics and compilations of physician accounts.
  </p>

  <p>
    More than a century later, modern researchers have continued that effort
    using structured outcome measures, clinical registries and electronic
    case data. The COVID-19 pandemic provides an opportunity to see what that
    documentation looks like in contemporary homeopathic practice.
  </p>

  <h3>COVID-19 and Individualized Treatment</h3>

  <p>
  One such effort involved the Homeopathy Help Network in the United States.
  A 2024 publication by Parker Pracjek, Alastair C. Gray and Denise Straiges
  reported on a five-month retrospective clinical audit of care provided
  during the spring and summer of 2020.
</p>

<p>
  The audit included 305 clients with positive or probable COVID-19 who
  received individualized homeopathic care through telehealth. Rather than
  judging their experience by a single measure, the researchers examined
  outcomes in four different ways: final health status, degree of recovery,
  response to individual remedy prescriptions and the time required for
  symptoms to resolve.
</p>

<p>
  The first measure looked at each client's overall status at final contact.
  <strong> Among the full group, 76.2% were recorded as having a
  positive outcome</strong>: 70% as fully recovered and 6.3%
  as partially recovered. Outcomes remained unknown for 21.8% of the
  cases because contact ended before a final outcome could be established.
</p>

<p>
  A second measure used a five-point Likert scale to describe the degree of
  recovery following homeopathic intervention.
  <strong> On this measure, 74.4% were recorded as resolved or much
  better</strong>—69.2% resolved and 5.2% much better.
</p>

<p>
  The researchers also examined something more specific to homeopathic case
  management: how clients responded to individual remedy recommendations.
  This analysis involved <strong>238 clients whose cases were followed through
  closure</strong>. Because an individual client could receive more than one
  remedy recommendation during the course of care, those 238 clients accounted
  for <strong>986 individual remedy recommendations</strong>. Of those
  prescription responses, <strong>83.4% were recorded as positive</strong>,
  while 14.4% showed no improvement and 2.3% were recorded as
  having a negative reaction to an individual prescription, which the authors
  described, from the perspective of homeopathic philosophy, as a minor,
  temporary aggravation of existing symptoms.
</p>

<div class="stat-card">
  <p class="stat-number">83.4%</p>

  <p class="stat-label">
    of individual remedy recommendations were recorded
    as producing a positive response
  </p>

  <div class="stat-divider"></div>

  <p class="stat-label">
    986 remedy recommendations among 238 clients
    followed through case closure
  </p>
</div>

<p>
  Finally, the researchers examined the speed of symptom resolution.
  <strong> 74.7% of cases were recorded as resolving within 21 days
  of the first intervention</strong>, including 29.2% that resolved
  within five days.
</p>

<p>
  The authors noted that the observed recovery times compared favorably with
  previously published data describing the return to usual health among
  nonhospitalized adults with COVID-19. Because the audit did not include
  its own untreated comparison group, however, that comparison provides
  context for the findings rather than a direct controlled comparison.
</p>

  <p>
  A second project approached the problem on an international scale.
  CLIFICOL created a cloud-based clinical case registry through which
  practitioners in multiple countries could systematically record their
  experiences treating patients during the pandemic.
</p>

<p>
  A 2023 analysis by Alexander Tournier, Yvonne Fok, Robbert van Haselen
  and Aaron To reported that, by the time the paper was submitted, the
  CLIFICOL registry contained 1,735 acute laboratory-confirmed or suspected
  COVID-19 cases. Among the cases evaluated for eligibility, 1,116 with at
  least two consultations yielded 1,606 prescription episodes for analysis.
</p>

<p>
  The registry documented a highly individualized pattern of prescribing.
  Practitioners used a large number of remedies and repertory rubrics, with
  considerable variation among countries. The authors described the project
  as a way to systematically collect and compare clinical experience and
  preserve the data for future analysis.
</p>

<p>
  The researchers also examined clinical outcomes using the
  <em> Outcome Related to Impact on Daily Living</em> (ORIDL) scale, a
  nine-point scale ranging from deterioration to complete recovery. They
  defined <em>rapid recovery</em> as major improvement by the third day
  following treatment, with no relapse or new prescription through day seven.
</p>

<p>
  Outcome data were available for 1,310 prescription episodes.
  <strong> Overall, 59% met the study's definition of rapid recovery. </strong>
  The highest proportion reported was <strong> 66% among patients whose symptoms
  had persisted for more than 30 days before homeopathic treatment began.</strong>
</p>

<div class="stat-card">
  <p class="stat-number">59%</p>

  <p class="stat-label">
    met the study's definition of rapid recovery
  </p>

  <div class="stat-divider"></div>

  <p class="stat-label">
    769 of 1,310 prescription episodes with outcome data
  </p>
</div>


<p>
  Together, these projects demonstrate the value of systematically preserving
  real-world clinical experience. Just as earlier physicians documented their
  experiences during the epidemics of their own eras, HHN and CLIFICOL created
  a modern record of individualized homeopathic care during COVID-19—now using
  structured outcome measures, electronic case data and international clinical
  registries. These records provide substantial practice-based evidence of how
  homeopathy was used and how patients progressed during care, while creating
  a foundation for further research.
</p>

</section>


        <!-- WHAT DOES IT TELL ME -->

        <section class="reflection-section">
          <h2>So What Does Two Hundred Years of Epidemic History Tell Me?</h2>

          <p>
            When I first encountered Julian Winston's writing on homeopathy in
            epidemics years ago, I was captivated by the history he brought
            together. Those stories helped convince me that homeopathy was
            something I needed to understand more deeply, and they stayed with
            me.
          </p>

          <p>
            Returning to this history now as a student of classical homeopathy
            has given me the opportunity to encounter many of these stories
            again—this time by following them into old books, medical journals,
            government reports, institutional records and other surviving
            documents.
          </p>

          <p>
            That process has given me an even greater appreciation for the
            historical record itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            I don't need every story repeated about homeopathy to be perfect.
          </p>

          <p>
            I want to know what the records actually say.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sometimes that means discovering additional context. Sometimes it
            means finding that a number has changed as it has been repeated
            over the years. And sometimes it means finding that an
            extraordinary account really does lead back to a remarkable
            contemporary document.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of that weakens my confidence in homeopathy.
          </p>

          <p>
            It strengthens it.
          </p>

          <p class="closing-emphasis">
            A system of medicine with more than two centuries of recorded
            experience does not need its history embellished. Its history is
            remarkable enough on its own.
          </p>

        </section>


        <!-- STORY STILL BEING WRITTEN -->

        <section>
          <h2>The Story Is Still Being Written</h2>

          <p>
            I am a student of this history, not its definitive historian.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are homeopaths who have devoted decades of scholarship and
            clinical work to epidemic disease, and anyone who wants to explore
            this subject more deeply should know their work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Jeremy Sherr, with Wenda Brewster O'Reilly—translator of
            Hahnemann's <em>Organon of the Medical Art</em>—has written {" "}
            <em>
              The Homoeopathic Treatment of Epidemics: Dynamics &amp;
              Methodology
            </em>.
            Their work explores collective disease, epidemic case-taking,
            analysis, repertorization and the development of the epidemic
            remedy in considerably greater depth than I can attempt here.
          </p>

          <p>
            André Saine has undertaken an extraordinary historical research
            project,{" "}
            <em>
              The Weight of Evidence—Homeopathy in Times of Epidemic and
              Related Statistics
            </em>.
            The Canadian Academy of Homeopathy describes the project as the
            result of reviewing more than 25,000 volumes, with a planned series
            exceeding 2,000 pages after the first 2,000 references and
            thousands of additional references still awaiting integration.
          </p>

          <p>
            And I remain indebted to Julian Winston, whose historical writing
            first introduced me to many of these stories years ago.
          </p>

          <p>
            What began for me as an astonishing collection of stories
            eventually became a much more interesting question:
          </p>

          <blockquote>
            What happens when I go back and read the records for myself?
          </blockquote>

          <p>
            I found government returns.
          </p>

          <p>
            I found hospital statistics.
          </p>

          <p>
            I found contemporary critics.
          </p>

          <p>
            I found investigations.
          </p>

          <p>
            I found old medical journals.
          </p>

          <p>
            I found institutional certificates written in another language.
          </p>

          <p>
            I found misinterpretations.
          </p>

          <p>
            And I found records that were even more remarkable when I finally
            saw where they came from.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is much more still buried in archives that I have not
            examined.
          </p>

          <p>
            But after more than two centuries, one thing is clear to me:
          </p>

          <p class="final-statement">
            The history of homeopathy in epidemic disease is not merely a story
            that homeopaths tell one another.
          </p>

          <p class="final-statement">
            It is a history that was written down.
          </p>

          <p class="final-statement">
            And it is still waiting to be read.
          </p>

        </section>


        <!-- HISTORICAL EVIDENCE NOTE -->

        <aside class="evidence-note">
          <p class="evidence-note-label">A Note About Historical Evidence</p>

          <p>
            Medical records from the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries were
            created before the development of modern randomized controlled
            trials, standardized diagnostic criteria and contemporary
            epidemiological methods. Statistics in this article are therefore
            presented as historical records and reported outcomes, not as
            modern estimates of treatment efficacy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whenever possible, I have traced figures back to primary or
            near-primary sources and have distinguished contemporary reports
            from later retellings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Modern studies discussed here should be evaluated according to
            their own research designs and limitations.
          </p>

          <p>
            My purpose is neither to transform historical observations into
            modern clinical trials nor to dismiss them because they arose
            before modern trial methodology.
          </p>

          <p class="evidence-question">
            It is simply to ask: What did the historical record actually
            report?
          </p>
        </aside>


